107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Blockchain is a decentralized and distributed ledger technology that stores transactions in a secure and tamper-resistant manner. Instead of relying on a single central authority, blockchain maintains a shared record of transactions across a network of participants. One of the important applications of blockchain technology is the use of smart contracts, which are programs deployed on a blockchain and executed according to predefined rules. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (In this experiment, a blockchain-powered web application is developed using the Solidity programming language on Remix IDE and integrated with the MetaMask Wallet. The experiment demonstrates how a user can create a Solidity smart contract, compile it, deploy it on a blockchain test network, and perform transactions through a MetaMask account. The given experiment specifically requires setting up MetaMask, connecting a testnet with Remix IDE, creating a simple smart contract based on the selected mini-project, compiling and deploying it, checking transaction details, and interacting with the deployed contract. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (MetaMask is a cryptocurrency wallet that provides an interface for users to interact with blockchain networks and decentralized applications. It manages blockchain accounts and allows users to approve and sign transactions. In this experiment, MetaMask is used as the wallet through which transactions are performed on the selected test network. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (First, MetaMask is installed as a browser extension and an account is created or imported. The wallet is then connected to a test network such as Sepolia Testnet or RSK Testnet. Test cryptocurrency is added to the wallet so that transactions such as smart-contract deployment and function execution can be performed. The experiment manual specifies Sepolia Testnet or RSK Testnet as the networks to be used. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (A testnet is a blockchain network used for development and testing purposes. It provides an environment similar to a main blockchain network but uses test cryptocurrency instead of real funds. This allows developers and students to safely deploy and test smart contracts. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (In this experiment, the smart contract is deployed on either the Sepolia Testnet or RSK Testnet. The use of a testnet makes it possible to perform transactions and observe blockchain behavior without using real cryptocurrency. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Remix IDE is a web-based development environment used to write and work with Solidity smart contracts. It provides facilities for creating Solidity source files, compiling smart contracts, deploying them to a blockchain network, and interacting with deployed contracts. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (A Solidity contract is written in Remix IDE and then compiled using the Solidity compiler. Compilation verifies the program and generates the information required for deployment and interaction with the contract. After successful compilation, the contract can be deployed using the Deploy & Run Transactions section of Remix. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Solidity is a programming language used to develop smart contracts for Ethereum-compatible blockchain platforms. A smart contract contains variables, functions, and rules that define how information is stored and how operations are performed. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 92 0 R
/E (Aim: To develop a blockchain-powered web application using Solidity programming language on Remix IDE and embedding Metamask Wallet )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (MetaMask can be connected to Remix IDE so that Remix can use the blockchain account managed by MetaMask. The appropriate injected MetaMask provider is selected in Remix, and MetaMask asks the user for permission to connect. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (After deployment, Remix provides an interface through which the functions of the smart contract can be accessed. Functions that modify the contract's stored information require a blockchain transaction and therefore require confirmation through MetaMask. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Other functions that only retrieve information from the blockchain can be called to observe the current state of the contract. By executing these functions, the user can verify that the smart contract has been deployed correctly and that the expected information has been stored or updated. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
120 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (After performing transactions, their details can be checked using a blockchain explorer. The experiment instructions specifically mention checking transaction details on the RSK Explorer. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
121 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 93 0 R
/E (Transaction details can be used to verify that the transaction was successfully processed. Depending on the network and explorer, information such as transaction hash, block number, sender address, receiver or contract address, transaction status, and gas information can be viewed. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
